Clear the high half of an unsigned int that x86-64 code reads from a whole
64-bit register: where it widens the value to 64 bits and where it converts
it to floating point. Keep the size of a VLA in size_t, as GCC does, so
that an index scales by it in the pointer's width, and scale an index into
a declared pointer to a VLA row by the row's size.

On a 64-bit target gen_cast emits nothing for a cast of a 64-bit value to
unsigned int, which leaves the value's high half in its register, and
nothing for the widening of an unsigned int to 64 bits, which takes the
high half for zero, as it is after a 32-bit load or a 32-bit operation.
A value cast down in a register and widened again kept its high half:
(unsigned long)(unsigned int)x, and x == (unsigned int)x, which the
kernel's USER_LEN32 tests, came out as x and as true. The widening of an
unsigned int on x86-64 now clears the high half with movl r, r. The
registers RC_INT names are rax, rcx and rdx, which need no REX prefix.

gen_cvt_itof converts an unsigned int with the 64-bit cvtsi2sd or
cvtsi2ss, and to long double with fildll of the whole register pushed, so
(double)(unsigned)(c ? a : 0) with a = 0x100000001 came out 4294967297.
It now clears the high half with movl r, r before either.

tcc kept the size of a VLA in an int slot and read it back as an int. An
index into a pointer to a VLA row was scaled by a 32-bit imul, whose
product the 64-bit add of the pointer took as zero-extended, so p + i
with i = -1 moved p almost 4 GiB forward; and a size of 0x80000000 or
more, which an int cannot hold, read back negative. The slot is now a
size_t, the product of a length and an element's size is a size_t, and an
element of fixed size is pushed as one, so that the index is widened to
the pointer's width, by movslq for a signed index and movl for an
unsigned one, before a 64-bit multiply. The difference of two pointers to
VLA rows divides by the size cast to ptrdiff_t, which keeps the quotient
signed. On i386 size_t is 32 bits wide and the code is the same.

gen_op took the runtime size only for an operand whose own type has
VT_VLA, which an array of variable length has and a declared pointer to
one, int (*p)[n], does not: p + 1 moved p by the size of a pointer and
p - q divided by it, on both targets. It now takes the runtime size when
the pointed type has VT_VLA too.

Every other use of a 32-bit value is a 32-bit instruction, a load or store
of 32 bits, or an argument or return value, whose high half the ABI leaves
undefined.

